\DOC X86_SUBROUTINE_SIM_TAC

\TYPE {X86_SUBROUTINE_SIM_TAC : ?is_safety_thm:bool -> thm * (thm * (thm option array)) * int * thm * thm -> term list -> int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges one call to a verified subroutine inside an
{eventually x86 ... s<n>} goal by applying a supplied correctness (or safety)
theorem for the callee, replacing the pre-call state assumptions with the
callee's post-state assumptions and advancing the symbolic state index.

\DESCRIBE
The arguments are: an optional flag {is_safety_thm : bool}, which when given
makes the supplied subroutine theorem be treated as a safety/event-trace
specification, additionally producing a side goal about the event list; a tuple
{(machinecode,execth,offset,submachinecode,subth) : thm * (thm * (thm option
array)) * int * thm * thm} consisting of the caller's machine-code definition,
its execution/decoding data (including the code-length theorem), the byte
offset at which the callee code sits inside the caller, the callee's
machine-code definition, and the correctness/safety theorem for the callee;
{ilist0 : term list}, the terms, read in the current state, used to instantiate
the callee theorem's universally quantified parameters (may be empty); and
{n : int}, the index of the current symbolic state, used to name the successor
state. The tactic instantiates the callee theorem with the terms in {ilist0},
matches it against the current state's register/memory assumptions, and
advances the state index, leaving the conclusion otherwise unchanged:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. ...) s<n>
   ==================================== X86_SUBROUTINE_SIM_TAC (machinecode,execth,offset,submachinecode,subth) ilist0 n
   A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. ...) s<n+1>
}
\noindent In {A'}, the pre-call facts about the state at index {n} (program
counter, stack pointer, argument registers, memory contents, event list, and
the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame) are removed and replaced by the
corresponding facts about the state at index {n+1}: the return program counter
and stack pointer, the callee's postconditions (results in memory or registers,
possibly as implications or universally quantified statements), the preserved
reads carried over, and an extended {MAYCHANGE} frame. Assumptions unrelated to
the machine state are kept. When the supplied theorem is a safety/trace
specification, an extra first goal is produced before the advanced {eventually}
goal:
{
   A ?- eventually x86 (\s'. ...) s<n>
   ==================================== X86_SUBROUTINE_SIM_TAC is_safety_thm (machinecode,execth,offset,submachinecode,subth) ilist0 n
   A ?- <current event list> = APPEND z1 <base trace>
   A' ?- eventually x86 (\s'. ...) s<n+1>
}
\noindent where {z1} is a fresh trace-tail variable.

\FAILURE
Fails when the supplied subroutine theorem cannot be matched against the
current goal, reported as a match failure from the internal {MATCH_MP_TAC}
step; in the failing cases the instantiated specification did not line up with
the goal's conclusion and state assumptions, whereas in the successful cases it
did. This can come either from the tactic's own matching of the goal or from
the shape of the theorem supplied as an argument.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only these two shapes, always on goals whose
conclusion is an {eventually x86} statement with a full set of machine-state
assumptions.

\ENDDOC
